PUBLIC RESPONSE: D. Hool

In the octosyllabic couplets of The Book of the Duchess Chaucer regularly closes a couplet on the forms that are given in A, B, C and E.

* wente – e.g. the past of *wen* ‘go’ is used as a normal couplet end, wente / shente etc.;
* here – here / there, dere / here etc. is a normal Middle-English couplet rhyme;
* fool – the poet is happy to end a couplet on *fool* and to bring it in with *cool*, *tool* etc.;
* countour / counteur – the Anglo-French ‘counter, reckoner’ is an end rhyme that Chaucer does use in the poem, e.g. with *trouver* / *mountour* type rhyme.

*Hool* is not an end-rhyme that Chaucer ever sets in The Book of the Duchess. The form is essentially a ghost form for Middle-English *hol/hool* ‘whole, hale’ and it never occurs as a rhyming terminus in the poem, whereas the other four forms do occur as rhyme words. Hence the only item in the list with which Chaucer does not make a rhyme in the work is Hool.

